1
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:16,200
ANN: Today is November the 6th, 1977. We mark this as the

2
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:23,380
201st birthday of Bethel and Lebanon churches. This is a very special

3
00:00:23,380 --> 00:00:30,420
day in the history of the church in that a special group of visitors will be coming to visit

4
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:37,480
the homestead and also to see the historical room and the cemetery at Bethel

5
00:00:37,480 --> 00:00:44,660
Church. These people are from other states. They are descendants of

6
00:00:44,660 --> 00:00:52,677
the youngest daughter of Oliver Miller. Her name was Mary Miller. She married William

7
00:00:52,677 --> 00:01:00,353
Wallace of Cross Creek and eventually moved into Ohio.

8
00:01:00,353 --> 00:01:07,360
This will be a special welcoming program for them today which will involve a tour

9
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:14,560
of the homestead and a tour of Bethel Church. This morning I am meeting with children of the Sunday

10
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:21,160
School to talk to them of the plans we are making to decorate the homestead for the Christmas holidays.

11
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:34,660
On November the 5th, 1776, a young Presbyterian

12
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:41,880
missionary minister by the name of John McMillan came into this

13
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:49,100
area to preach to the settlers that were here. Now these were pioneer people that

14
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:56,790
lived in log cabins deep in the woods, and they grew their crops of corn and rye and turnips. And

15
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:04,410
they were very isolated from the rest of the country because of the Allegheny Mountains. And

16
00:02:04,410 --> 00:02:11,890
we know that John McMillan stopped at the home of Oliver Miller. I

17
00:02:11,890 --> 00:02:19,470
hope all of you have visited the old stone house in South Park. That's where the log house to it, where

18
00:02:19,470 --> 00:02:26,810
Oliver Miller and his family lived. And Oliver Miller had a wife and ten children.

19
00:02:26,990 --> 00:02:33,990
There were six boys and four girls. And the youngest was a little girl by the name of

20
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:41,110
Mary. I want you to remember that. That's very important in the story. Now this is a scene

21
00:02:41,110 --> 00:02:48,110
that I made last year showing John McMillan, there he is in his black cape and his horse

22
00:02:48,110 --> 00:02:55,130
is tied back here to the fence. And maybe this is Mary Miller here giving him some grass to feed

23
00:02:55,130 --> 00:03:02,270
because the horse was probably very hungry because he traveled many miles. And when John McMillan got off

24
00:03:02,270 --> 00:03:05,130
of his horse, he took his saddlebags.

25
00:03:05,130 --> 00:03:12,290
and these are saddlebags that actually did belong to John McMillan. Now what do you suppose

26
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:19,350
he had in those saddlebags that he carried inside the log house? What do you think he had? Speak up real

27
00:03:19,350 --> 00:03:27,150
loud. Now you still won't go on. Real loud, though you're shouting. Come on. Like

28
00:03:27,150 --> 00:03:34,390
a Bible? Definitely, because we know he preached a sermon. An ink

29
00:03:34,390 --> 00:03:41,550
pen because he kept a journal, didn't he? He wrote down everything he did and we're very happy that he did

30
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:48,810
that because he wrote in his journal that on November the 5th 1776 he

31
00:03:48,810 --> 00:03:56,290
stopped at Peters Creek and preached in the home of Oliver Miller and baptized 5 children.

32
00:03:56,850 --> 00:04:04,010
And if any of you went last year to the service that we had over at the homestead in the summer, you remember that they baptized

33
00:04:04,010 --> 00:04:11,370
10 children at that time. And the reason why that was so important was that these were children

34
00:04:11,370 --> 00:04:18,990
that had been born in this area before there was any minister here. And you can imagine how excited

35
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:26,810
these people were that finally there was a minister that was going to come and preach to them the word of God. This

36
00:04:26,810 --> 00:04:34,010
was very, very important to them. Now, in those days, there were no stores. There were no places where people could

37
00:04:34,010 --> 00:04:43,050
buy things. Everything they made for themselves. And because of that, they learned to share. They

38
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:45,230
shared what they had with each other.

39
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:53,350
And I'm sure when the word got around that this young minister was coming, that every one of the wives in

40
00:04:53,350 --> 00:05:00,950
these pioneer families got busy and whatever little bit of food she had, she got busy and made something. So

41
00:05:00,950 --> 00:05:07,990
that after the service was over, they would all sit down and share the food together. And that's what we're going to

42
00:05:07,990 --> 00:05:15,235
do today over at the homestead. We have oat cakes made, and we have homemade bread, cornmeal mush, and what they call

43
00:05:15,235 --> 00:05:23,330
corn dodgers, which are like little pancakes fried on a skillet. Those will all be things in the kitchen. But

44
00:05:23,330 --> 00:05:30,390
the most exciting thing that's going on today is that we are going to have some special visitors at the

45
00:05:30,390 --> 00:05:39,970
homestead. Remember I said that the youngest daughter was a little girl by the name of Mary. Two

46
00:05:39,970 --> 00:05:47,110
years ago when we had a special holiday october festival a man came all the

47
00:05:47,110 --> 00:05:54,290
way from Columbus, Ohio, and his name is Preston Wolfe, and do you know what

48
00:05:54,290 --> 00:05:54,970
he told me.

49
